  "query_preview": "..\nKEY MESSAGE 1...\n\nWhile skies were slow to clear this morning, skies are now sunny. \nFair weather cumulus have developed but those will dissipate late \ntoday. Tonight, high clouds will move in from the north with \nclearing expected across north-central NY late tonight. Because of \nthis, the high clouds should help keep temperatures from dropping \noff sharply except where skies eventually clear. Temperatures were \nlowered slightly in the Tug Hill and Wyoming Valley. This resulted \nin some areas dropping into the 30s tonight, though not quite cool \nenough for frost. Fog is also looking unlikely tonight as conditions \nwill be too dry.\n\nHigh pressure will stay in place to start the weekend. Some periods \nof sunshine will be possible. Temperatures will be cooler than \ntoday, only climbing into the mid 60s to low 70s. \n\n \nKEY MESSAGE 2...\n\nA low pressure system will approach the region from the west \nSaturday night, potentially kicking off some isolated to scattered \nshowers ahead of it. Rain will become more widespread early Sunday \nmorning and continues throughout the day as the low moves across the \nregion. Some models are showing limited instability, so isolated \nrumbles of thunder cannot be ruled out despite low confidence in any \nstorms actually developing. WPC continues to highlight the region in \na risk for excessive rainfall. Looking at guidance, PWATs will peak \nbetween 1.75 and 2 inches. Warm cloud depths will near 12k feet. \nSteady light to moderate rainfall is expected but the high PWATs and \nwarm cloud depths are supportive for localized heavier showers. \n\nGuidance is spread on exactly where the highest rainfall totals will \nbe, though the general mean focuses on the Twin Tiers and into the \nCatskills. NBM guidance seems more reasonable during this update \nwith 1 to 2 inches of QPF from early Sunday morning through early \nMonday evening with pockets of higher totals of 2+ inches. The exact \nlocations of these higher amounts will depend on the system's track. \nThe Twin Tiers would be most vulnerable to hydro issues as soil \nmoistures are slightly above normal due to recent precipitation \nwhereas northern areas of central NY were rated abnormally dry in \nthe latest drought monitor. With all that said, confidence is low \nfor any flash flood issues through some ponding in urban and poor \ndrainage areas will be possible.\n\nThis low moves east of the region late Sunday night and drags a \nfront south of the region. Behind this system, high pressure brings \ndrier air back in for Monday. Being in zonal flow, some passing \nshowers will be possible across portions of northeast PA and the \nCatskills. Broad surface high pressure centered of eastern Canada \nwill then compete with additional disturbances Tuesday through \nThursday. An upper low will develop over the Great Lakes region \nearly in the week and still under zonal flow, showers will continue \nto move through the region. Just how far north these showers will be \nis uncertain with the high pressure to the north. The upper low will \nmove little by Wednesday. East to southwesterly flow will advect \nAtlantic moisture inland, supporting showers midweek. The upper low \nbecomes an open wave as a surface low develops over the Mid-Atlantic \ncoast. This low will slide up the coast with the next round of \nshowers on Thursday.\n\nThe first extended taste of fall is expected next week. Temperatures \nwill only max out in the 50s and 60s before falling into the 30s and \n40s at night.",
